% ============================================================================
% optic-focal-point-location — for three of the four basic optical
% elements, WHERE the SAME already-cited OpenStax sentence says the
% focal point sits relative to the element (adj-facts-stdlib, PHYSICS).
% ============================================================================
% A sibling to the already-shipped `lens-types.adj`
% (`optic_action(optic, action)`, whether each of four basic optical
% elements converges or diverges parallel light — convex_lens →
% converges_light, concave_lens → diverges_light, concave_mirror →
% converges_light, convex_mirror → diverges_light). That table's own
% header already quotes, verbatim, an OpenStax sentence for THREE of
% the four rows that states WHERE the focal point sits — a fact the
% action-only schema had no room for:
%
%   convex_lens → opposite_side_of_the_lens
%     "...intersect (or focus) at a single point on the optical axis on
%      the opposite side of the lens"
%
%   concave_mirror → same_side
%     "A ray approaching a concave converging mirror parallel to its
%      axis is reflected through the focal point F of the mirror on
%      the same side."
%
%   convex_mirror → behind_the_mirror
%     "A ray approaching a convex diverging mirror parallel to its axis
%      is reflected so that it seems to come from the focal point F
%      behind the mirror."
%
% Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? optic_focal_point_location(convex_lens, $Location)   % opposite_side_of_the_lens
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5, a TWO-column table): each
% `row` lowers to a ground relation `optic_focal_point_location(optic,
% location)` carrying the citation, so the lookup above is the ordinary
% SLD binding query — the engine returns the location WITH its source,
% on the CPU, with zero model calls, and abstains on anything that is
% not one of these three elements. `concave_lens` is deliberately NOT a
% row here: its own already-quoted OpenStax sentence ("all rays that
% enter it parallel to the optical axis diverge") describes the ray
% behavior but never states where the (virtual) focal point sits, so
% this sibling is HONESTLY NARROWER than its parent — it grounds
% exactly the three elements whose cited span states a location and
% abstains on the fourth, or any other word, rather than inventing one.
% This is genuinely new information beyond the parent's converge/
% diverge action: it distinguishes a REAL focal point (light physically
% passes through it — convex_lens's opposite side, concave_mirror's
% same side) from a VIRTUAL one (light only appears to originate from
% it — convex_mirror's behind_the_mirror), the core distinction needed
% to reason about real vs. virtual image formation. The query also
% runs BACKWARD — bind a location and recall which element has it:
%
%     ? optic_focal_point_location($Optic, behind_the_mirror)   % convex_mirror
%
% Provenance: reproduces, byte-for-byte, three of the four OpenStax
% sentences already quoted inside `lens-types.adj`'s own header — no
% new WebFetch, only a different schema decoding the location half of
% each already-verified quote. An ADJ `table` carries ONE provenance
% envelope (per `lens-types.adj`'s own convention), so `source`/
% `locator`/`trust` below hold the convex_lens span; the other two
% rows' own spans are listed above for independent auditability.
% `trust consensus` — the same tier `lens-types.adj` already carries
% (OpenStax via Physics LibreTexts, a widely used teaching resource,
% not a primary government/standards body).
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table optic_focal_point_location {
    columns optic, location

    row (convex_lens, opposite_side_of_the_lens)   % OpenStax: "...intersect (or focus) at a single point on the optical axis on the opposite side of the lens"
    row (concave_mirror, same_side)                % OpenStax: "...reflected through the focal point F of the mirror on the same side."
    row (convex_mirror, behind_the_mirror)         % OpenStax: "...reflected so that it seems to come from the focal point F behind the mirror."

    source "A convex or converging lens is shaped so that all light rays that enter it parallel to its optical axis intersect (or focus) at a single point on the optical axis on the opposite side of the lens"
    locator "https://phys.libretexts.org/Bookshelves/University_Physics/University_Physics_(OpenStax)/University_Physics_III_-_Optics_and_Modern_Physics_(OpenStax)/02:_Geometric_Optics_and_Image_Formation/2.05:_Thin_Lenses"
    trust consensus

    cites "A ray approaching a concave converging mirror parallel to its axis is reflected through the focal point F of the mirror on the same side." locator "https://phys.libretexts.org/Bookshelves/College_Physics/College_Physics_1e_(OpenStax)/25:_Geometric_Optics/25.07:_Image_Formation_by_Mirrors"
    cites "A ray approaching a convex diverging mirror parallel to its axis is reflected so that it seems to come from the focal point F behind the mirror." locator "https://phys.libretexts.org/Bookshelves/College_Physics/College_Physics_1e_(OpenStax)/25:_Geometric_Optics/25.07:_Image_Formation_by_Mirrors"
}
